<article class="env-lemma" id="04XD">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04XD">Lemma <span data-tag="04XD">100.3.3</span>.</a> Let $P$ be a property of morphisms of algebraic spaces as above. Let $f : \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a morphism of algebraic stacks representable by algebraic spaces. Let $W$ be an algebraic space and let $W \to \mathcal{Y}$ be surjective, locally of finite presentation, and flat. Set $V = W \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{X}$. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (f\text{ has }P) \Leftrightarrow (\text{the projection }V \to W\text{ has }P).  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The implication from left to right follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/04XC" data-tag="04XC">100.3.2</a>. Assume $V \to W$ has $P$. Let $T$ be a scheme, and let $T \to \mathcal{Y}$ be a morphism. Consider the commutative diagram 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix{ T \times _\mathcal {Y} \mathcal{X} \ar[d] &  T \times _\mathcal {Y} V \ar[d] \ar[l] \ar[r] &  V \ar[d] \\  T &  T \times _\mathcal {Y} W \ar[l] \ar[r] &  W }  \]
</div>
    <p>
       of algebraic spaces. The squares are cartesian. The bottom left morphism is a surjective, flat morphism which is locally of finite presentation, hence $\{ T \times _\mathcal {Y} V \to T\} $ is an fppf covering. Hence the fact that the right vertical arrow has property $P$ implies that the left vertical arrow has property $P$. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>